    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Marisa Shuman is a computer science teacher at a public middle school in New York City. Last year, she invited me to spend a few days in her classroom. She thought I might be interested in a lesson she had just created using ChatGPT. As a reporter who had spent years "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "covering",
            "ref": "v-cover"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " how technology is "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "reshaping",
            "ref": "v-reshape"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " education, I jumped at the chance."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Shuman used ChatGPT to create a lesson on wearable tech. She then tested the material with her 11th- and 12th-grade students. She told them that she didn’t mind if they learnt nothing about wearable tech. But she wanted them to carefully "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "examine",
            "ref": "v-examine"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " how "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "accurate",
            "ref": "v-accurate"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " and effective the lesson was. In other words, Shuman was using ChatGPT to build her students’ critical thinking skills."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Her students were freely critical. They found mistakes in the lesson, noted that it used numbers from advertisements, and pointed out that some questions didn’t make much sense."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The class reminded me why reporters can’t just sit at their desks. If I really wanted to understand how AI was changing education, I needed to visit more classrooms."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "I started by spending a morning at Khan Lab School in California. In one sixth-grade maths class, students were trying out “Khanmigo”, an AI-powered tutor. Some students asked it to explain maths problems in the form of a rap song. One student caught the bot making a mistake and proudly pointed it out."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "I also visited First Avenue Elementary School in New Jersey. I watched an AI bot walk third graders through some maths problems, step by step. School leaders told me they liked the AI tool but wanted students to think through the problem-solving steps themselves."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Then, on a recent trip to Washington, I met teachers who were using ChatGPT to design creative storytelling homework."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "What have I learnt from these visits? Well, it’s not so much about technology skills. Teachers everywhere told me the same thing: Students need to learn how to ask critical questions about AI. In some cases, this may be more important than knowing how to use the technology."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
